> ### 1. GLM Test and Plot

##### <u>**Logistic Regression Test Results (GLM):**</u>

```{r test_and_plot}
# a.
kf2 <- KidsFeet %>% 
  mutate(sex_dummy = ifelse(sex == "B",1,0))

# b.
myglm <- glm(sex_dummy ~ length, data=kf2, family=binomial)
summary(myglm) %>% pander()

# c.
pred <- predict(myglm, newdata = data.frame(length=25), type="response")

# d.
# > Calculate the e^beta_1
estimated_eb1 <- exp(myglm$coefficients["length"])
 
# e.
plot <- 
  (ggplot(kf2, aes(x=length, y=sex_dummy))+
    geom_point(aes(color = sex), size = 2)+
    # > This code below did this equation:
    # plot(Y ~ X, data=YourDataSet)
    # curve(exp(𝑏0+𝑏1*x)/(1 + exp( 𝑏0+𝑏1*x)), add = TRUE)
    # > "Just know, there is an equation actually graphing the line." - Bro. Saunders
    geom_smooth(method="glm", method.args = list(family="binomial"), se = FALSE, color = "grey80")+
    theme_test()+
    theme(plot.title = element_text(face="bold"),
          legend.position = "none")+
    scale_color_manual(values = c("skyblue2","hotpink2"))+
    labs(
      # "Practice `predict()` function using a GLM model",
      # subtitle = "Logistic Regression - W10",
      title = "KidsFeet Logistic Regression",
      x = "Foot Length",
      y = "Sex == \"B\""
    )
  )

# f.
x <- myglm$coefficients["length"]
log(exp(x))
```

---

##### <u>**Calculated Values:**</u>

* Predicted probability at x = 25: `r pred`
* $e^{\beta_1} =$ `r estimated_eb1`

---

##### <u>**Plot:**</u>

```{r show_plot, fig.height=3.5, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE}
plot
```


